    <meta name="dc.description" content="A comprehensive year-long characterization of PM2.5 collected during January-December 2017 at Srinagar (Garhwal) in the Central Himalayas, to investigate the seasonal variability, chemical composition, aerosol neutralization characteristics, and potential emission sources.. The annual mean PM2.5 concentration (80.01&#8201;&#177;&#8201;19.41&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3), exceeded both national and international air-quality guidelines, with maximum aerosol loading during winter and minimum concentration during the monsoon. Water-soluble ionic species accounted for approximately 41.3% of the PM&#8322;.&#8325; mass, with sulfate (7.36&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3) dominating the ionic composition, followed by chloride (6.81&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3), sodium (5.36&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3), and ammonium (3.13&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3), indicating the importance of secondary inorganic aerosols together with crustal and combustion-derived contributions. Neutralization analysis revealed that aerosols remained predominantly alkaline throughout the year, while chloride depletion analysis revealed extensive atmospheric aging of marine aerosols during long-range transport, with maximum depletion (33.91%) occurring during post-monsoon months coinciding with peak sulfate concentrations (13.55&amp;nbsp;&#956;g&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;3). Principal Component Analysis (PCA) suggested three probable source influences explaining 75.54% of total variance: long-range transported aged aerosols mixed with crustal dust (41.33%), combustion emissions (21.04%) and episodic agricultural and crustal influences (13.17%). These findings reflect the Central Himalayan foothills as a key transitional receptor of regional aerosols and provide a scientific basis for air-quality management in this environmentally sensitive region."/>
