                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>A comprehensive year-long characterization of PM<sub>2.5</sub> collected during January-December 2017 at Srinagar (Garhwal) in the Central Himalayas, to investigate the seasonal variability, chemical composition, aerosol neutralization characteristics, and potential emission sources.. The annual mean PM<sub>2.5</sub> concentration (80.01 ± 19.41 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>), exceeded both national and international air-quality guidelines, with maximum aerosol loading during winter and minimum concentration during the monsoon. Water-soluble ionic species accounted for approximately 41.3% of the PM₂.₅ mass, with sulfate (7.36 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>) dominating the ionic composition, followed by chloride (6.81 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>), sodium (5.36 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>), and ammonium (3.13 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>), indicating the importance of secondary inorganic aerosols together with crustal and combustion-derived contributions. Neutralization analysis revealed that aerosols remained predominantly alkaline throughout the year, while chloride depletion analysis revealed extensive atmospheric aging of marine aerosols during long-range transport, with maximum depletion (33.91%) occurring during post-monsoon months coinciding with peak sulfate concentrations (13.55 μg m⁻<sup>3</sup>). Principal Component Analysis (PCA) suggested three probable source influences explaining 75.54% of total variance: long-range transported aged aerosols mixed with crustal dust (41.33%), combustion emissions (21.04%) and episodic agricultural and crustal influences (13.17%). These findings reflect the Central Himalayan foothills as a key transitional receptor of regional aerosols and provide a scientific basis for air-quality management in this environmentally sensitive region.</p></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
